        {"after": "## 3. Binomial Nomenclature — The Naming System",
         "md": "**Deep dive — why two names?** A single local name can mean different plants in different districts; a single plant can carry many local names. Binomial nomenclature (Linnaeus, *Species Plantarum*, 1753) fixes this with one Latinised name of two words: the **generic name** (noun, capitalised) and the **specific epithet** (usually an adjective, lowercase), e.g. *Mangifera indica*. Both words are printed in italics (underlined when handwritten) and the author's abbreviated name may follow: *Mangifera indica* Linn. The name is governed by the International Code of Nomenclature (ICN), so the same organism carries the same name worldwide."},
